4\. Rate Limiting & CORS
========================

  

Rate limiting
-------------

  

Limits how many requests a client can make.

  

Example:

  

    100 requests / minute / user

  

Why?

  

  
*     
    
    Prevent abuse
    
      
    
  
*     
    
    Protect database
    
      
    
  
*     
    
    Prevent DDoS-like application exhaustion
    
      
    
  
*     
    
    Fair resource usage
    
      
    
  

  

Common algorithms:

  

    Fixed WindowSliding WindowToken BucketLeaky Bucket

  

### Redis example

  

    rate_limit:user:123

  

Redis is excellent for distributed rate limiting because multiple Node.js instances can share the same counter.

  

* * *

  

CORS
----

  

CORS = Cross-Origin Resource Sharing.

  

Suppose:

  

    Frontend:https://myapp.comAPI:https://api.myapp.com

  

The browser may block requests unless the API permits the origin.

  

Example:

  

http

    Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://myapp.com

  

Important interview point:

  

>   
> 
> **CORS is primarily a browser security mechanism. It is not an authentication mechanism.**
> 
>   

  

